<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oviedo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oviedo is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Founded around 761 under Fruela I, it was made the permanent royal seat by Alfonso II the Chaste in the early 9th century and remained the capital of the Kingdom of Asturias until the court moved to León in the early 10th century. Its cathedral and Cámara Santa hold relics that made it a stop on the pilgrimage roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oviedo.jpg" alt="Oviedo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_de_Oviedo_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> began in 720 with the revolt of the Visigothic aristocrat Pelagius against the Muslims who held most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. The hillside where Oviedo stands was then uninhabited, with no Visigothic or Roman settlement before it. It is said that two monks, Máximo and Fromestano, founded the city, and a small church dedicated to Saint Vincent soon followed. In 794 Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a> sacked and pillaged Oviedo in one of his campaigns against the Christian kingdoms.</p><p>Fruela I (757–768) was the first decided promoter of the city and built a palace and a nearby church. Alfonso II the Chaste (791–842) is credited with establishing it as a capital: the court was moved here from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravia">Pravia</a>, and the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> and a royal palace formed the nucleus of the city. His reign also saw the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados">San Julián de los Prados</a>. Ramiro I (842–850) added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco">Santa María del Naranco</a>, probably planned as a palace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-lillo">San Miguel de Lillo</a>, while facing rebellions by the Counts of the Palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oviedo is the capital of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and its administrative and commercial centre, with a population of 223,576. It lies between the Nalón and Nora rivers, about 24 km southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijon">Gijón</a>, and has a maritime climate. After the royal court moved to León following the death of Alfonso III in 910, the city's life stayed tied to the relics in its cathedral and to pilgrims travelling on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. The Cámara Santa, dating from 802 and attached to the cathedral, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and houses the Arca Santa, which holds the Sudarium of Oviedo.</p><p>The University of Oviedo was created in 1574 and inaugurated on 21 September 1608 under the will of Archbishop Fernando de Valdés Salas. In October 1934 about 50,000 workers, mostly miners, captured the city in the Asturian miners' strike, and troops sent by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> retook it, leaving about 3,000 rebels dead. The Siege of Oviedo in 1936 lasted three months, until the garrison was relieved in 1937. The city inspired the fictional Vetusta of Leopoldo Alas's novel La Regenta and hosts the Princess of Asturias Awards.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oviedo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs sent regular summer campaigns against the north: under Hisham I armies sacked <b>Oviedo</b> in 794 and 795 and raided Septimania in 793, though the Franks took Barcelona in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Fruela I founded <b>Oviedo</b> around 761, and Alfonso II later made it the permanent royal seat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When García died in 914 Ordoño II reunited most of the inheritance and made León his royal seat, and historians date the kingdom's name from this transfer of the capital from <b>Oviedo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
